## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical rollout discipline for CNFs, multi-region consistency, and robust rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary rollouts in CNFs across regions
- Policy drift detection and reconciliation
- Observability with eBPF, traces, and metrics
- Safe rollback criteria and traffic mirroring
- Per-region SLI targets and controlled rollout

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconcilePolicy(desired, runtime, driftThreshold) 
async function reconcilePolicy(desired, runtime){
  const drift = diff(desired, runtime);
  if(drift > driftThreshold) throw new Error('Policy drift');
  // apply reconciliation
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you define drift metrics and thresholds for CNFs?
- How would you test rollback reliability under sudden latency spikes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical end-to-end config provenance and policy enforcement for multi-tenant CNF platforms, a common production risk area beyond image provenance.

## Key Concepts
- Config payload signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SBOM attachment for config changes
- Admission control with Kyverno/OPA enforcing signature, SBOM, and tenant labels
- Canary namespace validation before rollout
- Per-tenant rollback mechanisms and status surfacing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo workflow: verify config update integrity before applying
async function verifyConfigUpdate(payload, signature, sbom) {
  const okSig = await verifySignature(payload, signature);
  const okSBOM = await validateSBOM(payload, sbom);
  const tenantOk = checkTenantLabel(payload);
  return okSig && okSBOM && tenantOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback semantics across tenants?
- How would you handle SBOM drift when multiple tenants update concurrently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical tenant-scoped access control pattern using existing CNF primitives (sidecars, Redis) without overengineering.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant identity via header
- Redis-backed authorization cache
- Safe proxying in a sidecar

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: sidecar filter
const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
const path = req.path;
redis.hget(`${tenant}:allowed`, path, (err, ok) => {
  if (!ok) res.status(403).send('forbidden');
  else forward(req);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you seed and rotate the allowlist safely?
- How to handle missing tenant or Redis latency?
